## Reflection

### 1. What was the hardest part of synchronization? Why?
The hardest part was thinking about deadlock. I was afraid that holding the lock while waiting on the Semaphore would hang the program forever. I learned that I must call `acquire()` without holding the lock, and call `release()` after releasing the lock. This taught me that the order of locking and waiting is more important than the lock itself, and a small mistake in order stops the whole program.

### 2. How did your understanding of Race Condition develop?
At first I thought a Race Condition only happens when there is an obvious error. After this assignment I understood it happens because of a `read - modify - write` operation without protection. Even if the code is one line like `count = count + 1`, two Threads can read the same value and one update is lost. Now when I see any shared variable I ask myself: does it need a lock?

### 3. What would you do differently next time?
Next time I will start with a small test for each feature alone before merging them all. In this assignment I did the three features one after another and it was hard to know where an error came from. If I had tested the first lock alone, then the second, then the Semaphore, it would have been easier to track errors and understand the effect of each feature on the output.

### 4. How does this assignment relate to real operating systems?
This assignment is the same idea as a real scheduler in an operating system. A real Ready Queue must be protected because more than one CPU takes from it, and the Semaphore is the same idea as `sleep/wakeup` in the kernel. I now understand why the OS does not let the CPU spin uselessly, because it wastes energy and time, and instead it puts it to sleep and wakes it when a new process arrives.

---

## Technical Answers

### 1. Race Condition in Your Output
In my original code without locks, an update to a counter could be lost. For example, two CPUs could execute `contextSwitchCount = tmp + 1` at the same time after reading the same `tmp`. In my output after protection, the value is `expected = observed` in every line of the verification. This proves the lock prevented lost updates.

### 2. Critical Sections and Lock Granularity
I used one lock `queueLock` for the whole Ready Queue because the list and map must change together as one unit. If I used two separate locks, one could add to the list without adding to the map. For statistics I used one lock `statsLock` because the critical sections are very short (just read and modify), so the loss in concurrency is small and the benefit is simplicity and deadlock prevention.

### 3. Busy Waiting vs Semaphore
Busy Waiting made the CPU loop in a `while` and check the queue continuously without benefit. In my new code the CPU calls `readyCount.acquire()` and sleeps until `add()` calls `release()`. The difference shows in my output: `Busy-waiting polls = 0`, meaning there was no useless spinning. This saves CPU time and makes the program calmer and faster.

### 4. Classical Problems and Deadlock
My problem is like the Producer-Consumer problem. The Main thread produces processes and the CPUs consume them. I prevented deadlock with three rules: first, never hold `queueLock` while waiting on the Semaphore, second, call `release()` after releasing the lock, third, on shutdown call `release(totalCPUs)` so all sleeping CPUs wake up and exit the loop instead of hanging forever.
